    "comparison_note": "1 of 23 training hour(s) exceed 40 ug/m3. The sigmoid on the regression's predicted NO2 gives 0.03 to 0.16 (in-sample Brier 0.046, leave-one-out 0.049); at >= 0.5 it catches 0 of 1 exceedance hour(s) with 0 false alarm(s). The logistic probability gives 0.02 to 0.11 (in-sample Brier 0.039); at >= 0.5 it catches 0 of 1 exceedance hour(s) with 0 false alarm(s). With only 1 hour(s) in the rarer class (a common rule of thumb asks for about 20, 10 per feature) the logistic model cannot learn a reliable boundary: its coefficients rest on 1 example(s) of the rarer outcome and its in-sample Brier score is flattered because it was fitted to exactly these labels; its probabilities barely move away from the base rate of 0.04. The sigmoid on the regression is the more trustworthy score here: it uses every measured NO2 value rather than a yes/no label, rises smoothly with predicted NO2 (0.5 exactly where the prediction reaches 40 ug/m3) and is explained by the regression's coefficients. It is still a score, not a calibrated probability: its steepness (0.2 per ug/m3) is chosen, not fitted, and it is only as good as a regression on 23 hour(s).",
